.Sh DESCRIPTION
Every xft container that owns memory takes a
.Vt t_allocator
by value and calls through its vtable.
A
.Vt t_allocator
pairs a pointer to a constant table of function pointers with an untyped
pointer to the backend state:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef void     (*t_free)(t_any alloc, t_buffer old);
typedef t_buffer (*t_reallocate)(t_any alloc, t_buffer old,
                     t_size new_size, t_size align);
typedef t_buffer (*t_allocate)(t_any alloc, t_size size,
                     t_size align);
typedef void     (*t_destroy)(t_any alloc);
typedef t_buffer (*t_clone)(t_allocator self, t_buffer buffer);

typedef struct e_alloc_vtable {
	t_free        free;
	t_reallocate  realloc;
	t_allocate    allocate;
	t_destroy     destroy;
	t_clone       clone;
} __attribute__((__aligned__(__GCC_CONSTRUCTIVE_SIZE)))
  t_alloc_vtable;

typedef struct s_allocator {
	_Alignas(__GCC_CONSTRUCTIVE_SIZE)
	const t_alloc_vtable *__restrict__  vtable;
	t_any                               allocator;
} t_allocator;
.Ed
.Pp
Each constructor below points
.Fa vtable
at a
.Ql static const
table defined inside it, so every
.Vt t_allocator
of one backend shares the same read-only table, and copying a
.Vt t_allocator
copies two pointers.
Entries are called as
.Ql allocator.vtable->allocate(allocator.allocator, size, align) .
The table cannot be changed per instance; to override an entry, define a
separate
.Vt t_alloc_vtable
and point
.Fa vtable
at it.
.Pp
The
.Fa alloc
argument of
.Fa allocate ,
.Fa realloc ,
.Fa free
and
.Fa destroy
is the
.Fa allocator
member.
The
.Fa self
argument of
.Fa clone
is the
.Vt t_allocator
itself, passed by value.
.Vt t_free ,
.Vt t_reallocate ,
.Vt t_allocate
and
.Vt t_destroy
are declared
.Ql nonnull
on their first argument.
.Pp
Allocations are
.Vt t_buffer
fat pointers.
A failed allocation is signalled by a
.Dv nullptr
.Fa mem
member; the
.Fa size
member may still be nonzero, so test
.Fa mem .
On success
.Fa size
is the usable size, which may be larger than requested.
A buffer handed back to
.Fa free
or
.Fa realloc
must be the
.Vt t_buffer
exactly as it was returned, since some backends use its
.Fa size
to find where it belongs.
.Pp
A
.Vt t_allocator
does not own its backend state.
The arena, GPA or report allocator passed to the constructor must outlive
every copy of the returned
.Vt t_allocator ,
and is released only by calling
.Fa destroy
or the backend's own destroy function.
.Pp
.Fn xft_arena_allocator ,
.Fn xft_gpa_allocator ,
.Fn xft_reporta_allocator
and
.Fn xft_page_allocator
build a
.Vt t_allocator
around one backend.
They allocate nothing and are declared
.Ql const ;
every call to one constructor returns the same
.Fa vtable
pointer.
The vtable entries map onto the backends as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "xft_reporta_allocator"
.It Fn xft_arena_allocator
.Fa allocate
calls
.Fn xft_arena_alloc
and returns a buffer of exactly
.Fa size
bytes.
.Fa realloc
resizes the block in place when
.Fa old Ns .mem
is aligned to
.Fa align
and the block either ends at the top of the arena's current page, with room
in that page for
.Fa new_size
bytes, or already holds
.Fa new_size
bytes.
A block at the top of the page moves the top to the new end, so shrinking it
releases the tail to later allocations.
Otherwise it allocates a new block of
.Fa new_size
bytes, copies the smaller of the old and new sizes, and leaves the old block
in place.
.Fa free
does nothing.
.Fa destroy
calls
.Fn xft_destroy_arena .
See
.Xr xft_arena 3 .
.It Fn xft_gpa_allocator
.Fa allocate ,
.Fa realloc ,
.Fa free
and
.Fa destroy
call
.Fn xft_gpa_alloc ,
.Fn xft_gpa_realloc ,
.Fn xft_gpa_free
and
.Fn xft_gpa_destroy .
See
.Xr xft_gpa 3 .
.It Fn xft_reporta_allocator
As above with the
.Fn xft_reporta_*
functions.
.Fa destroy
prints a usage report to standard error.
See
.Xr xft_gpa 3 .
.It Fn xft_page_allocator
.Fa allocate ,
.Fa realloc
and
.Fa free
call
.Fn xft_palloc ,
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
and
.Fn xft_palloc_free ;
.Fa align
is ignored.
.Fa destroy
is
.Dv nullptr .
The
.Fa allocator
member points to a static
.Vt t_page_alloc
that carries no state.
See
.Xr xft_palloc 3 .
.El
.Pp
All four set
.Fa clone
to
.Fn xft_alloc_clone .
.Ss Thread-safe allocators
.Vt t_ts_gpa ,
.Vt t_ts_reporta
and
.Vt t_ts_arena
pair a
.Vt t_mutex
with a
.Vt t_gpa ,
.Vt t_reporta
or
.Vt t_arena :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct s_ts_gpa {
	t_mutex    mutex;
	t_gpa      gpa;
} t_ts_gpa;

typedef struct s_ts_reporta {
	t_mutex    mutex;
	t_reporta  reporta;
} t_ts_reporta;

typedef struct s_ts_arena {
	t_mutex    mutex;
	t_arena    arena;
} t_ts_arena;
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn xft_new_ts_gpa ,
.Fn xft_new_ts_reporta
and
.Fn xft_new_ts_arena
return the backend built by
.Fn xft_new_gpa ,
.Fn xft_new_reporta
or
.Fn xft_new_arena
with the mutex unlocked.
.Pp
Each
.Fn xft_ts_*_alloc ,
.Fn xft_ts_*_realloc ,
.Fn xft_ts_*_free
and
.Fn xft_ts_*_destroy
function takes
.Fa alloc
as a pointer to the thread-safe struct, locks its mutex with
.Dv xft_lock_fast ,
calls the matching backend function on the inner allocator and unlocks the
mutex before returning.
.Fn xft_ts_arena_realloc
follows the arena vtable's
.Fa realloc .
.Fn xft_ts_arena_free
does nothing and takes no lock.
.Pp
.Fn xft_ts_gpa_allocator ,
.Fn xft_ts_reporta_allocator
and
.Fn xft_ts_arena_allocator
build a
.Vt t_allocator
whose vtable entries are those functions, with
.Fa clone
set to
.Fn xft_alloc_clone ,
which allocates through the locked
.Fa allocate
entry.
They allocate nothing and are declared
.Ql const .
A single
.Vt t_allocator
built this way may be shared between threads.
.Pp
Only these functions lock.
.Fn xft_arena_checkpoint ,
the rewind functions and direct calls on the inner
.Fa gpa ,
.Fa reporta
or
.Fa arena
member take no lock, and must not run while another thread uses the
allocator.
.Pp
.Fn xft_alloc_clone
allocates
.Fa buffer Ns .size
bytes by calling
.Fa self Ns .vtable->allocate
on
.Fa self Ns .allocator with an alignment of
.Dv __GCC_CONSTRUCTIVE_SIZE ,
and copies
.Fa buffer Ns .size
bytes from
.Fa buffer Ns .mem
into the new block.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The constructors return the filled
.Vt t_allocator .
.Pp
.Fn xft_alloc_clone
returns the new buffer as returned by the backend, so its size may exceed
.Fa buffer Ns .size .
On failure it returns a buffer with
.Fa mem
set to
.Dv nullptr
and
.Fa size
set to 0.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

__attribute__((__nonnull__(1), __noreturn__))
void	xft_main(const t_any *const __restrict__ sp)
{
	t_gpa		gpa;
	t_allocator	allocator;
	t_buffer	memory;
	t_buffer	copy;

	(void)sp;
	gpa = xft_new_gpa();
	if (gpa.slab == nullptr)
		xft_exit_process(1);
	allocator = xft_gpa_allocator(&gpa);
	memory = allocator.vtable->allocate(allocator.allocator, 1000, 64);
	if (memory.mem == nullptr)
	{
		allocator.vtable->destroy(allocator.allocator);
		xft_exit_process(1);
	}
	xft_pin_invariant(memory.size >= 1000);
	copy = allocator.vtable->clone(allocator, memory);
	if (copy.mem != nullptr)
		allocator.vtable->free(allocator.allocator, copy);
	allocator.vtable->free(allocator.allocator, memory);
	allocator.vtable->destroy(allocator.allocator);
	xft_exit_process(0);
}
.Ed
